@@ -0,0 +1,200 @@
+# Initial-lifetime scoped disclosure (local candidate)
+
+This opt-in API implements **logical, whole-message commitment to an immutable
+old context**. It is not a native-write cutoff, a published SDK qualification, or
+proof that sensitive POS bindings are safe. The ordinary session/RPC APIs do not
+inherit this claim. This local candidate must remain separate from production
+sensitive routes until combined review and the required tests/qualification pass.
+
+## Exact boundary
+
+`claimInitialDisclosureLifetime()` claims an SDK session's initial, authenticated,
+trusted, restricted physical epoch exactly once. Concurrent claims and claims
+through delegating wrappers contend for the same actual SDK instance. Session IDs,
+peer maps, fingerprints, and generation numbers alone cannot create a lifetime.
+
+Delegation is not identity attestation. Immediately after obtaining the lifetime,
+the application protocol must require `lifetime.belongsTo(suppliedSession)` before
+reading/trusting that session's virtual identity or incoming stream or sending an
+application handshake. This final opaque-authority method uses reference identity
+with the actual SDK instance; it calls no wrapper property, callback, or equality.
+A wrapper that forwards a genuine claim/send but substitutes another identity or
+incoming stream is therefore rejected. On mismatch, seal the obtained lifetime
+and reject without fallback; do not invoke the unverified wrapper's cleanup code.
+The ownership fact remains true for the real SDK instance even after seal and is
+not proof of live authority or application authentication.
+
+The SDK rejects an unrestricted, enrollment-only, unauthenticated, legacy,
+previously rearmed, retiring, or already-claimed session. It also rejects a session
+on which a legacy application send has already been admitted, even if that send
+is still queued or failed. Ordinary `send`/`sendAtGeneration` are denied after the
+claim; file transfer is unavailable in the required restricted profile. SDK
+protocol Ping/Pong/Close may still run. Third-party session defaults fail closed;
+there is no unscoped compatibility fallback.
+
+Each ticket owns a nonempty, metadata-free `P2pMessage.Binary`. The binary already
+snapshots its payload and metadata; callers cannot mutate that message after
+preparation. Application encoding and authorization happen before preparation.
+Preparation does not commit disclosure. A send claims its one-shot ticket, waits
+for the core send mutex, validates the exact original target/epoch, then checks
+the deadline and seal under the lifetime's short authority mutex. That last
+critical section is the **logical commitment** of the entire encoded application
+message. Protocol framing/envelopes/encryption occur afterward on the captured
+target; the sender never looks up a current peer or replacement connection.
+
+`seal`, uncommitted cancellation, outbound commitment, and local publication
+commitment serialize on that same authority mutex:
+
+| Winning order | Allowed result |
+| --- | --- |
+| Seal/cancel/expiry before message commitment | No application message from that ticket may be written. |
+| Whole-message commitment before seal | That entire message may begin or finish transmitting after seal, **only on its original target**. |
+| Seal/cancel/expiry before publication commitment | Publication is denied. |
+| Publication commitment before seal | Completion is allowed only into its captured original pending/application context, even after seal. |
+
+This deliberately permits later old-context delivery. It cannot hide an already
+committed result from someone retaining the old endpoint keys, recall a value
+already returned to an application, or make a global/latest-user queue safe. A
+new employee, login, purpose, or application authority needs a genuinely fresh
+physical/application lifetime. Even A-to-A reconnect does not reopen the old one.
+
+## Authoritative application obligations
+
+The capability is **not authentication or business authorization**. A strict
+application protocol must:
+
+1. Claim and verify exact instance ownership with `belongsTo` before its first
+   application Hello/Ready or any other application send;
+   use the scoped path for every application handshake and message. Never upgrade
+   an existing legacy application channel, collector, or pending-work queue.
+2. Bind one immutable authenticated principal, device/session, purpose, and
+   projection context to the lifetime. The core does not infer employees from
+   authenticated device fingerprints and does not add receive-epoch provenance
+   to legacy `incoming` messages.
+3. Authenticate the **original request credentials** on every request/duplicate,
+   not whichever employee is current when an async check completes. Buffered
+   prelogin/old-channel messages must not acquire a new caller's authority.
+4. Reauthorize each result/projection, including failures and recovered results,
+   against that immutable context immediately before preparing/committing its
+   disclosure or publication. A durable operation ID is not a result capability.
+5. Synchronously call and **await `seal()` on every affected lifetime before an
+   authoritative account/session/device/owner/projection-policy replacement
+   becomes active**. Delayed observers, expiry timers, logout broadcasts,
+   cancellation, and cleanup callbacks are not this ordering. Coordinate this
+   boundary with the actual authoritative application policy transaction.
+6. Use application-level authenticated operation recovery after ambiguity. Never
+   resend a one-shot ticket or attach old pending results to a new employee.
+
+The first strict RPC candidate is invoke-only, with its own handshake/dialect and
+immutable application context. Transport controls and notifications are disabled
+universally there, not just for methods currently marked sensitive. Applications
+must not enable a legacy fallback or bypass this rule with a raw send.
+
+## Deadlines (not timers)
+
+Capture `deadlineAfter(ms)` **before** asynchronous authorization, decoding, or a
+callback that calculates the allowed validity. The maximum 30,000 ms is merely a
+library ceiling, not a grant of business authority. Narrow it with
+`deadline.limitTo(ms)` based on the earliest applicable credential/policy expiry.
+Narrowing preserves the original monotonic start and takes the minimum of the old
+and new allowance; it performs no clock read and cannot renew validity. A policy
+calculation after initial capture may therefore expire the request conservatively
+early. Nonpositive remaining validity must be rejected, never rounded up.
+
+The same original deadline is supplied to later `prepare` or
+`preparePublication`; do not recapture it after a suspended authorizer returns or
+while recovering/caching a response. SDK commitment checks elapsed monotonic time
+even when no timer ran. Wall-clock rollback cannot extend it. A regressing SDK
+monotonic clock permanently seals the lifetime. Actual policy changes still need
+the authoritative seal transaction; a TTL alone is not revocation.
+
+**Required production gate: device-suspend timing is unqualified.** The existing
+Android/JVM `System.nanoTime` and iOS `NSProcessInfo.systemUptime` clock sources
+are not uniformly proven suspend-inclusive. A prepared/precommit operation may
+resume after device sleep before this uptime-based allowance expires even though
+real credential expiry passed. A synthetic wall-clock rollback test does not
+qualify that case. Suspend-inclusive/native timing qualification is unavailable
+in this candidate; no sensitive POS binding or real credential-expiry-across-sleep
+claim is permitted until a reviewed suspend-inclusive clock or authoritative
+pre-resume retirement design is implemented and qualified. A delayed resume
+observer is not such an authority gate. No native transport change is made here.
+
+## Publication ownership
+
+`preparePublication(deadline)` reserves bounded metadata for one logical local
+publication decision. `tryCommit()` returns `true` at most once and executes no
+application callback or continuation. It owns no decoded application value.
+Callers must capture and retain the original pending/application context before
+asynchronous work, and use the decision immediately before completing only that
+context. Scheduling between the decision and completion is permitted by the
+logical contract; looking up `currentEmployee`, a new pending record, or a shared
+global queue in that gap is prohibited. Sealing cannot recall previously exposed
+or returned data. A `false` decision is terminal for that attempted publication.
+
+## Ownership, bounds, and cleanup
+
+- Constructors for lifetimes, tickets, publications, and deadlines are internal;
+  there are no serializers or copy/send-right minting APIs. Public state values
+  are advisory snapshots, not authorization proofs or collectable flows.
+- At most 32 prepared/in-flight messages/publications and 8 MiB of policy
+  allowance are retained per lifetime. Each entry costs 512 bytes plus its binary
+  size (publication entries have no binary). The restricted profile's shared
+  `PayloadBudget` is additionally charged; protocol encoding has its own budget.
+- Expired uncommitted entries are reclaimed lazily on gate operations. Seal and
+  cancellation release uncommitted payload ownership. Committed send ownership
+  remains until the Kotlin send invocation exits, even after seal or expiry.
+- The authority gate runs only library-owned state and a monotonic-clock check.
+  It executes no app callback, continuation, transport I/O, or suspension.
+  Acquiring it may suspend. Observable shared-budget reservations/releases occur
+  outside it, and lifecycle shared-budget release notifications also occur outside
+  the outer connection lock.
+- Callback-free lifetime critical sections do not imply callback-free lifecycle
+  return progress. Unlocking the lifetime mutex can resume a queued Unconfined
+  caller inline after the authority is already sealed/unlocked, while a core
+  lifecycle caller still owns the outer connection lock. That caller's later app
+  code can delay lifecycle completion or another connection-lock user. Existing
+  state observers have related outer-lock liveness limits. This is not a later
+  disclosure right; there is no hard return-time/cleanup-progress guarantee.
+- Core loss, rearm capture, local Closing, or terminal claim permanently retire
+  the lifetime **before** associated cleanup/publication. Rearm-in-progress
+  cannot claim while the public generation still says one/Connected. Failed or
+  cancelled rearm does not restore the disclosure authority.
+- `seal()` mutates the authority non-cancellably; it does not wait for sendMutex,
+  transport shutdown, native callbacks, worker cancellation, or acknowledgement.
+  A committed writer may still be active when seal completes.
+- `Finished` means a committed Kotlin send invocation exited, successfully or
+  exceptionally. It is not success, receipt, native completion, or native buffer
+  release. Caller-held objects, wire buffers, OS memory, and native retention are
+  outside the lifetime's policy allowance. Failure/cancellation after commitment
+  remains ambiguous and never creates a retry right.
+
+Invalid API shape/deadline ownership/allowance uses `IllegalArgumentException`;
+payload and retention failures use the existing typed size/protocol errors.
+Unavailable lifetime/expiry admission uses authorization refusal. Wrong session,
+unsupported claim, and ticket reuse use connection refusal; a ticket already
+invalidated before send admission may likewise report connection refusal. Do not
+derive retry authority or delivery evidence from these error classes.
+
+Expected-refusal preparation and deadline-validation APIs carry Kotlin
+`@Throws(Exception::class)` for the Swift error boundary, as the session claim and
+send APIs already do. Actual exported-header and Swift runtime qualification is
+still required; common/JVM assertions do not prove that interop boundary.
+
+## Evidence boundary
+
+The regression sources cover primitive authority ordering/bounds, paused
+precommit and send-mutex cases, first-epoch/rearm/terminal integration, real
+protocol multichunk old-target behavior, and JVM thread races. These are
+in-memory/source regression checks, not physical-device or native-buffer proof.
+Four additional `ScopedRpcSdkIntegrationTest` cases and their test-only core-test
+to RPC-main dependency are preserved in Ukkera's separate, unapplied SDK-test
+overlay. Both JVM and Android test sources compiled, but neither runtime suite
+could execute because strict locks reject three new dependency memberships.
+The base candidate does not contain or count those four tests. No partial lock
+rewrite, shadow source, friend-path or unlocked-classpath workaround is used.
+The complete supported lock/API maintenance workflow and an actual integration
+test run remain required; a source-reviewed overlay is not executed evidence.
+Only actual recorded combined build/test results qualify the candidate; source
+authorship or review alone does not resolve the application-level F13 finding.
+No native transport changes or Apple cancellation/native-completion guarantees
+are part of this contract.
