+This is a bounded candidate in the isolated Ukkera source checkout, not the official rc6 release.
+The original upstream checkout is not modified. See the checkout's `UKKERA-LOCAL-PROVENANCE.md`
+and core's [logical disclosure contract](../p2p-core/SCOPED-DISCLOSURE.md).
+
+## Separate opt-in surface
+
+- `ScopedRpcHostSession.create` and `ScopedRpcClientSession.create` accept an already connected,
+  authenticated, trusted, **restricted-profile actual SDK `P2pSession`**.
+- The SDK's exclusive initial lifetime is claimed **before any application binary, including HELLO**.
+  Its final opaque `belongsTo(session)` attestation then checks referential ownership before RPC
+  reads the supplied session's identity/incoming stream. A delegating wrapper cannot substitute them.
+  Mismatch seals the captured lifetime and refuses; it never calls an unverified wrapper's cleanup.
+- Unscoped/default/third-party, previously used, retired, rearmed, wrapped or already-claimed sessions
+  cannot fall back to ordinary `send`, generation sending, legacy RPC, pairing, public routes or login.
+  A session manager returning the same cached object for the same peer does not make it fresh.
+- `ScopedRpcProcedure<Q,R,E>` is **not** `RpcProcedure<Q,R,E>`. Neither registration nor calling has
+  a conversion, retry-safety flag, notification API, public/bootstrap flag or fallback error handler.
+- The explicit `PSRP` outer profile contains version 1, invoke-only capability 1, reserved zeros and
+  a SHA-256 binding digest. Its framed binding is supplied independently at **both** endpoints, not
+  learned from a HELLO or copied from a caller assertion. Legacy `PRPC` framing is unchanged.
+
+The host must already be listening before the client negotiates. There are at most twenty nonsecret
+HELLO attempts within one five-second deadline; no response/downgrade means negotiation fails. This
+is not application retry. Neither facade creates sockets, chooses a newer peer link or reconnects.
+
+## Application-owned authentication and authoritative seal ordering
+
+The immutable `ScopedRpcBinding` identifies scope, actor, application session, purpose and projection.
+These are bounded **nonsecret identifiers**, not credentials or authorization proofs. Device key trust
+does not authenticate an employee. A trusted application adapter must:
+
+1. Calculate a conservative window no longer than the earliest applicable original credential,
+   challenge and policy expiry. The SDK captures its clock **before** this calculation; `limitTo`
+   narrows that origin and cannot refresh it. Zero/exception denies; the hard ceiling is 30 seconds.
+2. Authenticate the **actual decoded Q** on every host original and duplicate. Verify the original
+   credential/challenge, principal, target, purpose and projection against independently trusted state.
+   The proof must remain valid throughout the supplied window. A remembered login Boolean, claimed
+   actor ID, device pin, request hash or library-generated binding does not replace this proof.
+3. Return `ScopedRpcRequestGrant(binding, disclosureKey)` only after that verification. The key is
+   nonsecret original resource/projection/policy-cut metadata, never raw Q, credentials or a mutable
+   authorization object. The returned binding must equal the locally configured binding.
+4. Implement current `Request`, `Response` and client `Publication` disclosure authorization using
+   the **original** request/grant metadata. Defaults deny. Exceptions/timeouts do not yield an error
+   route with weaker checks. Business errors and infrastructure status disclosures use the same gates.
+5. Serialize relevant authority replacement and runtime activation. **Await seal on every affected
+   local runtime before making replacement scope/account/device/session/ownership/projection/credential
+   authority active**, and use genuinely fresh physical sessions for replacement runtimes. Timers,
+   logout notifications and post-change observers are not this ordering. Legacy `RpcTrust.onRevoke`
+   is not an atomic policy-transition fence and must not be advertised as one.
+
+**Real credential expiry across device sleep is not qualified.** Current JVM `nanoTime` and Apple
+`systemUptime` timing are not proven suspend-inclusive. No-timer tests exercise an advancing logical
+clock, not OS sleep. A production expiry claim requires qualified suspend-inclusive deadline timing
+or independently authoritative retirement before resumed disclosure; timers or advisory callbacks
+alone do not close this gate. Sensitive-route binding remains blocked.
+
+There is no in-place token refresh, enrollment upgrade or bootstrap-to-employee promotion. If a future
+application registers login/refresh, it still needs actual original-Q proof and current response
+authorization in a fixed challenge lifetime. A challenge reply cannot upgrade that physical lifetime.
+
+The library does not supply employee credential storage, authoritative policy, command transactions,
+recovery provenance or a durable exactly-once operation mechanism. A handler already admitted in its
+old context can complete effects after revocation/cancellation; applications must separately authorize
+and fence durable command commits. RPC cancellation is not transaction rollback.
+
+## Host dispatch, duplicate handling and disabled controls
+
+- The receive boundary captures the SDK deadline before parsing Q or suspending authentication.
+  Unknown procedures, malformed Q, wrong bindings, failed proof and disabled messages have no
+  unauthenticated application response or record mutation.
+- Only fresh typed-Q authentication **and** current Request authorization allow a dedup lookup.
+  A duplicate must match request ID, procedure/version, library-computed body digest and the original
+  grant. Fresh duplicate proof never replaces saved original context or grant metadata.
+- An authenticated matching running duplicate may receive gated `Running`; a completed duplicate may
+  receive its gated cached result. Its own incoming pre-auth deadline can govern that duplicate reply,
+  but cannot extend the original handler/completion deadline.
+- Tombstones last for the entire physical lifetime. There is no TTL, silent expiry or capacity reset
+  that permits reexecution. Capacity exhaustion refuses fresh work after proof/current checks. Cache
+  exhaustion drops only bodies; cache size zero still retains tombstones and authenticates duplicates
+  before returning gated `ResultUnavailable`. Application durable operation IDs remain necessary.
+- `Status`, `Cancel`, `Receipt` and `Notify` are dropped **before binding/incarnation/record lookup or
+  mutation**. They do not cancel a handler, evict a result, query existence, publish a notification, or
+  indirectly close the RPC runtime. Malformed application packets are dropped, not weaker fallbacks.
+  All pairing/approval messages and unsupported dispatch kinds are also ignored.
+- Running/final/cached/application-error/generic-failure replies each require current Response
+  authorization with original grant metadata before preparing a full immutable scoped send ticket.
+
+## Client calls and actual publication
+
+Each call has one RPC request ID and exactly one Invoke attempt. There is no status recovery,
+reinvocation, transport cancel/receipt, reconnect reuse or notification flow. Recovering an ambiguous
+business operation requires a **new authenticated application operation-result RPC** with a new RPC
+request ID and the original durable business operation identity. This candidate does not implement
+that application procedure.
+
+The client captures one deadline before window calculation, encoding and Request authorization. That
+same origin governs queued send, response authorization, decoding and eventual value publication.
+Incoming response time never starts a new disclosure window. A result is tied to one original pending
+request, descriptor, binding and physical target. Duplicate/wrong-context replies cannot replace it.
+
+`call` returns `ScopedRpcDelivery`, **not a raw result or a shared result stream**. The handle retains
+the decoded-payload allowance and call slot. Its suspending one-use `take(currentPresentationBinding)`:
+
+1. Requires the caller's independently obtained **current** presentation binding. Do not blindly pass
+   back `delivery.binding`; that would defeat the application's context check.
+2. Freshly checks Publication authorization using original metadata and the original call deadline.
+3. Prepares and commits the SDK's same-gate publication decision immediately before transferring the
+   value into that original caller context. A queued handle cannot renew an expired window, even if
+   an expiry coroutine/observer never ran.
+
+`discard()` and seal clear untaken values; registration/take/discard are fenced by permanent RPC-owned
+state, not an advisory `isSealed` check alone. In-progress non-cooperative authorization retains its
+slot/data allowance until it actually exits and cannot later register or publish into a new context.
+Pending reply channels use cancellation/drain with owned-body release, not `close()` retaining data.
+Execution-status exceptions also require current publication commitment before their remote status
+can escape; local sanitized rejection/cancellation is not a remote execution receipt.
+Only the exact engine-created exception after that commitment preserves remote execution evidence.
+Serializer-supplied failures cannot assert a request ID, phase or safe-replay claim. These faults use
+the original generated call ID and SDK send-ticket evidence, with trusted Encoding/Decoding phases.
+
+Once the application takes a value, the library cannot recall it or prevent deliberate copying into
+another employee's UI. The app must keep original-context presentation ownership too.
+
+## What commitment and cleanup mean
+
+The SDK authority serializes logical whole-message/publication commitment with permanent seal. Exact
+fully encoded bytes and the immutable old transport target are selected before the send queue. There
+is no late encoding against new context, late peer-map lookup, mutable grant or compatibility send.
+
+**A message committed before seal may begin or finish transmitting after seal, only on its OLD target.**
+A publication commitment that wins before seal may finish only in its old caller context. This is not
+per-chunk/native-handoff cutoff, old-key secrecy, remote receipt, native completion or data recall.
+Call failure evidence comes from SDK commitment, not entry into an RPC worker. Uncommitted tickets
+are cancelled before reporting `NotSent`; committed failures are conservatively ambiguous.
+
+`seal()` is permanent and independent of native cleanup or handler/writer cooperation. `close()` seals
+first and then calls the same original SDK session's retained cleanup; repeated close reobserves a
+previous failure rather than claiming success because RPC state is already sealed. No worker joins
+itself and no app callback or native wait runs under the SDK commitment gate.
+
+## Bounded candidate, not a capacity qualification
+
+| Policy | Per physical runtime |
+|---|---|
+| Executing host work / outstanding client calls including deliveries | 1–8, default 4 |
+| Concurrent host authentication/handler/response attempts | At most twice the call limit; no business queue |
+| Lifetime host records | Calls through 1,024, default 128 |
+| Preparing/queued/in-flight RPC sends | 1–24, default 16 |
+| Request/result/application-error encoded body | At most 65,536 bytes each |
+| RPC retained-payload policy | 64 KiB–16 MiB, default 8 MiB |
+| Result cache | Zero through min(payload budget, 4 MiB), default 1 MiB |
+
+The profile separately budgets incoming/parser scratch, retained decoded Q, reserved result encoding,
+record metadata, cached bodies, prepared outgoing binaries, pending replies and retained decoded
+deliveries. Core also applies its own shared restricted-session budget and entry limits. Small policy
+budgets may refuse even individually legal bodies because multiple allowances coexist. These are
+conservative policy allowances, **not heap/RSS/native buffer measurements**. Non-cooperative work
+keeps its owners/slots until actual exit; cancellation does not falsely free data still in use.
+
+## Verification boundary
+
+New common-test sources cover explicit profile rejection, default refusal, actual-Q authentication
+before duplicate lookup, original-grant preservation, conflicts, running/completed/cache-zero behavior,
+disabled controls, lifetime exhaustion, pre-auth/same-origin expiry without timers, immutable targets,
+one-shot sends, queued cancellation, committed ambiguity, pending drain, delayed delivery publication,
+presentation changes, late non-cooperative authorization, resource faults and cleanup reobservation.
+
+**These tests were authored, not run by the RPC author.** The internal fake link is a deterministic
+logical-gate model, not an injectable public authority or native evidence. Lead-owned combined
+compilation/tests, actual core/facade interoperability, independent security review, Android/JVM and
+Apple/platform qualification, hostile-network testing and application-authority adapters remain gates.
+Nothing here claims 128-client service qualification, POS checkout readiness, authenticated realtime
+notifications, full transport controls, durable recovery, native cutoff or production approval.
